"Displays estimated signal power by frequency to identify recurring cycles in a regularly sampled series.\n\nUse when: A time series may contain periodic components not obvious in the time domain. Sampling frequency and observation duration support meaningful spectral resolution.\n\nAvoid when: Irregular sampling or aliasing is ignored, or the sample is too short for the claimed period.\n\nVisual grammar: Horizontal position encodes frequency or its reciprocal period. Vertical position encodes spectral power or power density with stated normalization and units.\n\nBuild: Confirm a regular sampling interval and choose detrending and windowing policies. Compute the periodogram with a named normalization. Plot frequencies through the valid range and annotate candidate peaks with their implied periods.\n\nCheck: Check Nyquist frequency follows the actual sampling rate. Verify frequency resolution is consistent with record length. State whether values are power, power density, or a logarithmic transformation.\n\nPitfalls: Spectral leakage can spread power into nearby frequencies and create misleading shoulders. A peak does not establish a stable mechanism; compare the time series and inspect sampling artifacts.\n\nExample: Analyze synthetic hourly demand for daily and weekly components, labeling cycles per hour and equivalent periods.\n\nAlternatives: Autocorrelation plot, Seasonal subseries plot, Line chart."
